"""Expand existing fire-inspection purchase owner without creating another URL."""
from refresh_dscr_purchase_methods import refresh

BODY='''<p class="lead">Before closing on an STR, identify the actual fire and life-safety inspection process for the address and intended use. Obtain the findings, price unresolved corrections, assign who completes them and establish what issued evidence permits the planned opening. A general home inspection, seller equipment list or appointment confirmation does not answer those separate questions. Do not fund an acquisition that only works if unapproved sleeping space becomes rentable.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Bring the inspection findings and correction-acceptance register to an acquisition call</a>. BNB Accelerator assists within its contracted acquisition scope; the authority and qualified independent professionals determine applicable requirements and safety.</p>
<h2>Identify the authority and required process first</h2>
<p>Verify the property boundary, responsible department, intended rental use and proposed layout with the appropriate local authority. Ask for the current applicable checklist, application sequence, inspection scope, unresolved findings and acceptance documents. A postal address or nearby cabin's permit is not proof that the same authority or rules apply. Ask how ownership changes, planned alterations and the intended guest capacity affect the actual process.</p>
<p>A geographically limited example is the <a href="https://www.seviervilletn.org/467/Short-Term-Rentals">City of Sevierville's short-term-rental permit page</a>. It describes documentation, payment and a Fire Department life-safety inspection as parts of its operational permit process, with annual renewal. Its <a href="https://www.seviervilletn.org/466/Codes-and-Inspection">Codes and Inspection page</a> tells applicants to schedule the STR inspection after applying and paying for the permit, and to verify city limits. These are City of Sevierville instructions, not Sevier County requirements or a national sequence. Confirm current address-specific directions before relying on them.</p>
<p>An application, scheduled inspection, completed visit, correction invoice and issued approval are different evidence. Ask the authority which documents establish acceptance for this property and use; do not invent a universal pass certificate or guaranteed appointment interval. A seller's older approval is a record to reconcile, not an automatic determination of the buyer's eligibility or a changed layout.</p>
<h2>Build a findings-to-acceptance register</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Evidence to request</th><th>Purchase decision</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Address, use and inspection scope</td><td>Current authority instructions, layout and identified reviewer</td><td>Does the review cover what the buyer intends to operate?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Actual unresolved finding</td><td>Dated report, affected location and required correction</td><td>What remains unknown or limits planned opening?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Correction and exclusions</td><td>Qualified contractor scope, accepted quote and omitted work</td><td>Does the price cover the finding rather than merely equipment?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Responsible party and payment</td><td>Written allocation, permissions and invoice due dates</td><td>Who can arrange access and fund the work?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reinspection or acceptance</td><td>Authority's required next step and issued resolution evidence</td><td>Is installed work accepted, or still awaiting review?</td></tr>
<tr><td>Opening and transaction deadlines</td><td>Current unresolved conditions, signed purchase protections and operating plan</td><td>Can the buyer lawfully open and fund a delay?</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Potential review questions include exits and rescue access, alarm systems, extinguishers, address identification, posted occupancy and other locally applicable features. This is a buyer's request list, not an exhaustive code checklist or design specification. Have the authority and qualified professionals identify the requirements for this building; do not install, disable or certify life-safety systems from a blog.</p>
<p>A quote marked complete can exclude electrical work, design, permits, access preparation or later corrections. Ask the contractor to identify those exclusions against the actual finding. Keep authorized evidence in secure channels; avoid sharing guest identities, access codes or detailed security information in public worksheets. Photographing an installed device or paying an invoice does not replace required inspection acceptance.</p>
<h2>Keep supported capacity separate from a repair promise</h2>
<p>Consider a hypothetical basement bunk room that the seller counts as additional sleeping space. An unresolved exit or other safety finding means the buyer should not assume the intended use is supported. Ask the authority and local professionals what the issue requires and whether another documented layout is actually permissible. There is no automatically lawful fallback capacity established by removing a few beds.</p>
<p>The <a href="/blog/certificate-of-occupancy-str-remodel/">remodel approval register</a> separately addresses proposed alterations and completion records. The <a href="/blog/parking-occupancy-str-cap/">capacity-reconciliation owner</a> separately addresses other binding constraints. Passing one review does not override rental permission, association restrictions, parking, septic, lender or insurer conditions. Reconcile these findings rather than treating the fire visit as approval of the entire investment.</p>
<p>Prepare a purchase case based on independently supported use, with full property costs and sourced revenue assumptions. Label proposed added capacity as unresolved until the needed evidence exists. If current lawful operation is unverified, a lower-revenue sensitivity cannot establish legality. An affluent buyer's available capital does not turn an unsupported room or uncertain approval into a sound base forecast.</p>
<h2>Worked correction and delay cash bridge</h2>
<p>All figures below are hypothetical arithmetic, not local fees, contractor estimates, inspection timelines, revenue forecasts or client outcomes. Assume $280,000 of spendable acquisition capital. Down payment, closing and basic setup allocate $210,000, with a separate protected $35,000 operating reserve: $245,000 total and $35,000 unassigned. Basic setup excludes every correction, review fee and additional carrying bill modeled below.</p>
<p>Assume distinct corrective work costs $9,000, separate professional and inspection-related bills cost $1,000, and an additional two-month unopened interval costs $2,000 monthly. Incremental cash is $14,000. Total allocation becomes $259,000 and unassigned margin $21,000. No STR receipts are assumed during that interval. The chosen interval is a sensitivity, not the authority's promised turnaround.</p>
<p>For a stress case, replace the work allowance with $21,000, the separate fees with $2,000 and the additional carrying interval with four months at $2,000 monthly. Incremental cash is $31,000; allocation becomes $276,000, leaving $4,000. Replace the original allowances rather than adding four months on top of two or counting the same invoice in both work and fees.</p>
<p>Now assume an unrelated $8,000 roof correction, excluded from all previous amounts. Allocation becomes $284,000, creating a $4,000 shortfall without consuming the protected reserve. A pure sensitivity in which a valid settlement adjustment reduces actual buyer cash by exactly $5,000 would bring allocation to $279,000 and leave $1,000. That is not a lending rule or guaranteed reimbursement: obtain the lender and closing professional's actual permitted treatment and payment dates.</p>
<p>Prepare a dated bill schedule, not just a total. A seller's promise to finish or a later reimbursement cannot pay the buyer's earlier contractor deposit unless an actually authorized funding arrangement exists. Use the <a href="/tools/first-str-purchase-budget/">complete purchase-budget worksheet</a> and keep household or business liquidity outside this property's allocation. The reserve is already allocated once, not an additional recurring expense or freely unassigned project money.</p>
<h2>Decide before protections expire</h2>
<p>Proceed when the actual acceptance evidence, supported use and complete cash schedule fit the acquisition. Reprice a viable property when verified correction and timing costs change the economics. Delay only within available signed protection while a decisive answer can realistically arrive. Decline a purchase that depends on unidentified correction scope, unavailable cash or unsupported guest capacity. Counsel determines remedies, notice requirements and any refund rights; this is not a contingency clause.</p>
<p>If work continues after closing, document who has authority to arrange it, what must happen before occupancy and how the buyer funds the no-receipts interval. Do not advertise an opening date based solely on a contractor estimate. Reconcile the findings again if the floor plan, possession date or proposed rental use changes. The <a href="/blog/permit-pending-due-diligence-extension/">permit-extension decision guide</a> asks whether additional pre-closing time can actually resolve the decisive uncertainty.</p>
<p><a href="/apply/">Discuss the shortlist with an address-specific correction memo</a>: finding, independent reviewer, accepted scope, completion evidence, cash deadline and stopping decision. The <a href="/blog/buy-str-high-income-large-tax-bill/">high-income acquisition roadmap</a> connects that memo to the wider purchase process. No expected tax benefit substitutes for the required safety or operating evidence.</p>
<p class="small">City of Sevierville primary pages reviewed October 7, 2026, geographically limited as labeled. BNB Accelerator is the interested acquisition-service publisher. This is document-based educational analysis, not a fire inspection, engineering specification or safety certification. Not legal, engineering, insurance, lending, tax or personalized investment advice. All amounts are hypothetical. No inspection result, rental permission, capacity, settlement adjustment, opening date or investment return is guaranteed.</p>'''

FAQ=[
('Does a general home inspection replace an STR fire inspection?', 'Do not assume so. Ask the actual authority which process and scope apply to the address and intended use, and obtain the required findings and acceptance evidence.'),
('Does paying for fire-safety corrections prove the property passed?', 'An invoice or installed equipment does not replace required acceptance. Confirm the applicable reinspection or issued resolution with the responsible authority.'),
('Can a buyer assume the seller\'s fire approval transfers?', 'No transfer rule is asserted. Ask how ownership, intended use and layout changes affect the actual requirements, and separately verify other operating restrictions.'),
('How should inspection delays change STR purchase cash?', 'Budget distinct correction work, separate review costs and additional carrying bills on their due dates. Replace overlapping allowances once and do not rely on unopened STR receipts.')]

if __name__=='__main__':
    refresh('str-fire-inspection-closing','STR Fire Inspection: Resolve Findings Before Closing','What Should an STR Fire Inspection Cover Before Closing?','Before buying an STR, verify fire-inspection findings, accepted corrections and opening evidence. Test correction costs and delay cash before closing.',BODY,FAQ,'2026-09-23','data-fire-acceptance-review-link','Use the <a href="/blog/str-fire-inspection-closing/">fire-inspection acceptance register and correction-delay cash worksheet</a> before relying on an opening date or unsupported sleeping space.')
